## DESCRIPTION
## Linear Models
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## Tagged by LD

## DBsubject(Algebra)
## DBchapter(Linear equations and functions)
## DBsection(Applications and models)
## Institution(ASU)
## MLT(LineFromPoints)
## Level(4)
## KEYWORDS('equation', 'line')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'contextFraction.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Fraction');

$ea = random(10000, 12000, 10);
$eb = random(10000, 15000, 10);
$ya = random(1980,  1990);
$yb = random(1991,  2001);
$yc = random(2002,  2020);

$enroll = (($eb - $ea) / ($yb - $ya)) * ($yc - $ya) + $ea;
$t0     = $yb - $ya;
$t1     = $yc - $ya;
$m      = Fraction($eb - $ea, $t0);

BEGIN_PGML
Teaneck High had an enrollment of [$ea] in [$ya] and an enrollment of [$eb] in year [$yb].
What is the predicted enrollment of Teaneck High in [$yc] if we assume a linear model?

	Predicted Enrollment = [_]{$enroll}{40}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Let's use the variable [`t`] to be the number of years after [$ya].  Since the model is to be linear we can write it as [`f(t) = m t + b`], where [`f(t)`] is the enrollment in the high school in year [`t`]. 

We also use the data that [`f(0) = [$ea]`] and [`f([$t0]) = [$eb]`], where [`t=[$t0]`] is used because [$yb] is [$t0] years after [$ya]. 

The first point results in [`b=[$ea]`] and then the second point can result in 
[```f([$t0]) = m \cdot [$t0] + b = m \cdot [$t0] + [$ea] = [$eb]```]. 

This results in 
[``` m = \frac{[$eb]-[$ea]}{[$t0]} = [$m] ```]

To find the predicted enrollment in [$yc], we note that [$yc] is [$t1] years after [$ya] and 
[``` f([$t1]) = m \cdot [$t1] + b = [$m] \cdot [$t1] + [$ea] = [$enroll] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();  